[{:db/id 17592186105701 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a12537f5-cc57-59f7-b432-1391f413c86b" "question-24948042-4caf-54ad-85ae-20d1f75949ff" "question-8e5a64ce-22c5-5e8c-997a-c2e3826ce849" "question-30aa8aaa-a9f8-52a5-8c3a-51fa1949b560"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105702 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-35d9ba0b-2c2a-5791-9dcc-879b93c51511" "question-c0976e49-e373-59e5-b244-6a3f86c33d78" "question-66d2028c-7755-5929-9531-f282efb6ee05"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105703 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-25a56934-09b0-592a-b002-4f360029a2ea" "question-fc72b4b3-1726-5899-88e5-9bd9e302a6a9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105480 :question/problem "Let ![](images/98/984a6c85f7337efcad03af6e6b7af44340243770ade690e383c286154ca9024c.svg) and ![](images/82/825255386c414868f28d139cdc3e50a2b8252fc0c6ad09de579119092afadb9c.svg). Find the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a12537f5-cc57-59f7-b432-1391f413c86b" :question/id #uuid "a12537f5-cc57-59f7-b432-1391f413c86b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126506" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,c\\,|=16$, $|\\,d\\,|=4$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta ={60}^{∘}.$ Find the vector projection of $c$ onto $d$ in terms of the vector $d.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $d:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{d}{|\\,d\\,|}=\\frac{d}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}d\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{d}\\,c & ={\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c\\cdot u \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}d \\\\  & =2d\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0478e591-82c4-56f2-9e83-eb51b18b93e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0478e591-82c4-56f2-9e83-eb51b18b93e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "0478e591-82c4-56f2-9e83-eb51b18b93e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d281791a-6600-51ff-a350-02e5807be308" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a927a06b-c1ab-5420-bff4-edf63dc12fe1" "answer-d281791a-6600-51ff-a350-02e5807be308" "answer-8ab5504e-47f2-5106-b694-7a718d03210a" "answer-d2a38c14-4542-5cbc-a049-1870202ed396" "answer-4898f0e7-39a5-5f06-8e56-8f5aafed918a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a927a06b-c1ab-5420-bff4-edf63dc12fe1" :answer/id #uuid "a927a06b-c1ab-5420-bff4-edf63dc12fe1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d281791a-6600-51ff-a350-02e5807be308" :answer/id #uuid "d281791a-6600-51ff-a350-02e5807be308" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ab5504e-47f2-5106-b694-7a718d03210a" :answer/id #uuid "8ab5504e-47f2-5106-b694-7a718d03210a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2a38c14-4542-5cbc-a049-1870202ed396" :answer/id #uuid "d2a38c14-4542-5cbc-a049-1870202ed396" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4898f0e7-39a5-5f06-8e56-8f5aafed918a" :answer/id #uuid "4898f0e7-39a5-5f06-8e56-8f5aafed918a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106480 :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,c\\,|=12$, $|\\,d\\,|=6$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c$ in terms of the vector $c.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =|\\,d\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $c:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|}=\\frac{c}{12}=\\frac{1}{12}c\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & ={\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d\\cdot u \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}c \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}c\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105028 :answer-field/correct 17592186105031 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-24948042-4caf-54ad-85ae-20d1f75949ff" :question/id #uuid "24948042-4caf-54ad-85ae-20d1f75949ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-316438" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,x\\,|=9$, $|\\,y\\,|=6$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\pi .$ Find the vector projection of $x$ onto $y$ in terms of the vector $y.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{y}\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$y$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{y}\\,x & =|\\,x\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $y:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{|\\,y\\,|}=\\frac{y}{6}=\\frac{1}{6}y\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{y}\\,x & ={\\text{comp}}_{y}\\,x\\cdot u \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42723228-7b18-5999-8f79-63b014252842"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42723228-7b18-5999-8f79-63b014252842" :answer-field/id #uuid "42723228-7b18-5999-8f79-63b014252842" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0ae82f23-3f33-55f8-b143-c4e2aa44358a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ae82f23-3f33-55f8-b143-c4e2aa44358a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ae82f23-3f33-55f8-b143-c4e2aa44358a" :answer/id #uuid "0ae82f23-3f33-55f8-b143-c4e2aa44358a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8e5a64ce-22c5-5e8c-997a-c2e3826ce849" :question/id #uuid "8e5a64ce-22c5-5e8c-997a-c2e3826ce849" :question/math-academy-id "q-275227" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,a\\,|=16$, $|\\,b\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b$ in terms of the vector $b.$\n\n${\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}\n$b$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $b:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}=\\frac{b}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}b\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}b \\\\  & =-4\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d497f000-d7bf-588c-ad25-93aa61c11659"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d497f000-d7bf-588c-ad25-93aa61c11659" :answer-field/id #uuid "d497f000-d7bf-588c-ad25-93aa61c11659" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3a7d4837-c858-547a-810b-4a932bb463f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3a7d4837-c858-547a-810b-4a932bb463f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a7d4837-c858-547a-810b-4a932bb463f5" :answer/id #uuid "3a7d4837-c858-547a-810b-4a932bb463f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-30aa8aaa-a9f8-52a5-8c3a-51fa1949b560" :question/id #uuid "30aa8aaa-a9f8-52a5-8c3a-51fa1949b560" :question/math-academy-id "q-101538" :question/problem "Suppose that $|\\,c\\,|=\\sqrt{2}$, $|\\,d\\,|=6$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c$ in terms of the vector $c.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the scalar projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =|\\,d\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $c:$\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|}=\\frac{c}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}c\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the vector projection as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & ={\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d\\cdot u \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}c \\\\  & =3c\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eb9967eb-bff2-51e3-a27a-d037cf218913"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb9967eb-bff2-51e3-a27a-d037cf218913" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb9967eb-bff2-51e3-a27a-d037cf218913" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e3dc05d9-0a8c-59cb-960f-32824025a367" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f759721-d199-5792-92d1-a10cf5a4cae6" "answer-915f2842-9a51-5c6a-addd-28d24f144fd1" "answer-b52a555e-441d-5b2d-97f8-a2ce1ea1b1a3" "answer-e3dc05d9-0a8c-59cb-960f-32824025a367" "answer-9b92b1e4-60cc-5678-839d-c81bf0c39ab7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f759721-d199-5792-92d1-a10cf5a4cae6" :answer/id #uuid "4f759721-d199-5792-92d1-a10cf5a4cae6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-915f2842-9a51-5c6a-addd-28d24f144fd1" :answer/id #uuid "915f2842-9a51-5c6a-addd-28d24f144fd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b52a555e-441d-5b2d-97f8-a2ce1ea1b1a3" :answer/id #uuid "b52a555e-441d-5b2d-97f8-a2ce1ea1b1a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3dc05d9-0a8c-59cb-960f-32824025a367" :answer/id #uuid "e3dc05d9-0a8c-59cb-960f-32824025a367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b92b1e4-60cc-5678-839d-c81bf0c39ab7" :answer/id #uuid "9b92b1e4-60cc-5678-839d-c81bf0c39ab7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-35d9ba0b-2c2a-5791-9dcc-879b93c51511" :question/id #uuid "35d9ba0b-2c2a-5791-9dcc-879b93c51511" :question/math-academy-id "q-101545" :question/problem "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $d$ onto $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{c\\cdot c}c \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}c \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 3+5\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 4}{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}c \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{26}⟨1,5,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{7}{26},-\\frac{35}{26},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b1da517-04d4-5359-b129-b49ab64ce95a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b1da517-04d4-5359-b129-b49ab64ce95a" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1da517-04d4-5359-b129-b49ab64ce95a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-23855489-4a31-5f1e-b065-d3158a58159b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2ffe9f6-07c0-5c84-9abc-3456ff2d160a" "answer-23855489-4a31-5f1e-b065-d3158a58159b" "answer-2ff64492-2e92-58ae-94ff-14ee9bcf8e67" "answer-6b914fd7-39d8-59b4-904b-c85d9957296a" "answer-b1eccb6d-008e-58b0-a553-ceff849b38ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2ffe9f6-07c0-5c84-9abc-3456ff2d160a" :answer/id #uuid "c2ffe9f6-07c0-5c84-9abc-3456ff2d160a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{1}{26},-\\frac{5}{26},0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23855489-4a31-5f1e-b065-d3158a58159b" :answer/id #uuid "23855489-4a31-5f1e-b065-d3158a58159b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{7}{26},-\\frac{35}{26},0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ff64492-2e92-58ae-94ff-14ee9bcf8e67" :answer/id #uuid "2ff64492-2e92-58ae-94ff-14ee9bcf8e67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{7}{29},-\\frac{35}{29},0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b914fd7-39d8-59b4-904b-c85d9957296a" :answer/id #uuid "6b914fd7-39d8-59b4-904b-c85d9957296a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{29},\\frac{5}{29},0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1eccb6d-008e-58b0-a553-ceff849b38ea" :answer/id #uuid "b1eccb6d-008e-58b0-a553-ceff849b38ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{7}{26},\\frac{35}{26},0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c0976e49-e373-59e5-b244-6a3f86c33d78" :question/id #uuid "c0976e49-e373-59e5-b244-6a3f86c33d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-19213" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $b$ onto $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 14+2\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨3,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{13},0,\\frac{2}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bba96ab3-dc17-5fca-8684-49c3032dcc63"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bba96ab3-dc17-5fca-8684-49c3032dcc63" :answer-field/id #uuid "bba96ab3-dc17-5fca-8684-49c3032dcc63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5f7f0f17-57fa-52b7-993d-3b6b9a55e712" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47726351-7b47-5626-bb7b-c86df88ec37f" "answer-c53001d4-9eb2-5ad7-92c6-63c38e3c778d" "answer-77159c13-4761-5c51-8623-db6f3303931c" "answer-5f7f0f17-57fa-52b7-993d-3b6b9a55e712" "answer-6ce967b6-1408-5524-8f74-a23b08c8dddb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47726351-7b47-5626-bb7b-c86df88ec37f" :answer/id #uuid "47726351-7b47-5626-bb7b-c86df88ec37f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,0,0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c53001d4-9eb2-5ad7-92c6-63c38e3c778d" :answer/id #uuid "c53001d4-9eb2-5ad7-92c6-63c38e3c778d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{13},0,\\frac{3}{13}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77159c13-4761-5c51-8623-db6f3303931c" :answer/id #uuid "77159c13-4761-5c51-8623-db6f3303931c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{11},0,\\frac{2}{11}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f7f0f17-57fa-52b7-993d-3b6b9a55e712" :answer/id #uuid "5f7f0f17-57fa-52b7-993d-3b6b9a55e712" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{13},0,\\frac{2}{13}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ce967b6-1408-5524-8f74-a23b08c8dddb" :answer/id #uuid "6ce967b6-1408-5524-8f74-a23b08c8dddb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{2}{13},0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-66d2028c-7755-5929-9531-f282efb6ee05" :question/id #uuid "66d2028c-7755-5929-9531-f282efb6ee05" :question/math-academy-id "q-19212" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨2,1,6⟩$ and $q=⟨4,2,12⟩.$ Find the vector projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{q\\cdot q}q \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}q \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 12}{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{12}^{2}}q \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{164}⟨4,2,12⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨4,2,12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e210b2c8-4214-56d5-9adf-4f596e3021f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e210b2c8-4214-56d5-9adf-4f596e3021f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "e210b2c8-4214-56d5-9adf-4f596e3021f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a859db1a-197e-54ad-9642-eb6e745f3fe5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0b5a1709-5c52-5848-bfee-e95ed0c3f88a" "answer-ac6f6de5-2f46-5d39-ac3f-cbdff716176a" "answer-a859db1a-197e-54ad-9642-eb6e745f3fe5" "answer-ce5ad26e-2d08-547a-8436-202ea22b7ccf" "answer-086de454-2713-5601-ab51-799550e139d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b5a1709-5c52-5848-bfee-e95ed0c3f88a" :answer/id #uuid "0b5a1709-5c52-5848-bfee-e95ed0c3f88a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,0,0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac6f6de5-2f46-5d39-ac3f-cbdff716176a" :answer/id #uuid "ac6f6de5-2f46-5d39-ac3f-cbdff716176a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,6,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a859db1a-197e-54ad-9642-eb6e745f3fe5" :answer/id #uuid "a859db1a-197e-54ad-9642-eb6e745f3fe5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,1,6⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce5ad26e-2d08-547a-8436-202ea22b7ccf" :answer/id #uuid "ce5ad26e-2d08-547a-8436-202ea22b7ccf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨4,2,12⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-086de454-2713-5601-ab51-799550e139d7" :answer/id #uuid "086de454-2713-5601-ab51-799550e139d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨6,2,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-25a56934-09b0-592a-b002-4f360029a2ea" :question/id #uuid "25a56934-09b0-592a-b002-4f360029a2ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-212463" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ x \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 11+4\\cdot x+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 4x+6 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-454a546d-b2cd-5e75-93bf-898cf6aab9d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-454a546d-b2cd-5e75-93bf-898cf6aab9d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "454a546d-b2cd-5e75-93bf-898cf6aab9d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c48bc586-6836-5bb9-bad5-ad9ded9e0a14" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70d47c6a-00e4-5617-9606-a3844ed20adc" "answer-332eee39-a81f-58e2-9df9-1c8788ae1cd9" "answer-c48bc586-6836-5bb9-bad5-ad9ded9e0a14" "answer-866002cd-f732-5473-af59-74d28524c347" "answer-27703fdc-dcae-5810-af67-bcf1ab7b205c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70d47c6a-00e4-5617-9606-a3844ed20adc" :answer/id #uuid "70d47c6a-00e4-5617-9606-a3844ed20adc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-332eee39-a81f-58e2-9df9-1c8788ae1cd9" :answer/id #uuid "332eee39-a81f-58e2-9df9-1c8788ae1cd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c48bc586-6836-5bb9-bad5-ad9ded9e0a14" :answer/id #uuid "c48bc586-6836-5bb9-bad5-ad9ded9e0a14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-866002cd-f732-5473-af59-74d28524c347" :answer/id #uuid "866002cd-f732-5473-af59-74d28524c347" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27703fdc-dcae-5810-af67-bcf1ab7b205c" :answer/id #uuid "27703fdc-dcae-5810-af67-bcf1ab7b205c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fc72b4b3-1726-5899-88e5-9bd9e302a6a9" :question/id #uuid "fc72b4b3-1726-5899-88e5-9bd9e302a6a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-212469" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the projection of $a$ onto $b$ is the zero vector, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals the zero vector only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot x+(-4)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =4x-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 4x-24 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =24 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-acb824ea-82ae-5e97-acfd-078323166d84"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-acb824ea-82ae-5e97-acfd-078323166d84" :answer-field/id #uuid "acb824ea-82ae-5e97-acfd-078323166d84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ee90094-51ba-5008-b5eb-89abf0073014" :answer-field/choices ["answer-192f30d7-4e5b-597c-b16e-383b0b641a0d" "answer-cbf3bc6d-a5c0-5131-be8c-1f828fbf1aa2" "answer-efe3cfb8-92c2-5e3a-b874-10f960d8adca" "answer-8aba8f71-12dd-51aa-a5d5-f2bf983e166b" "answer-9ee90094-51ba-5008-b5eb-89abf0073014"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-192f30d7-4e5b-597c-b16e-383b0b641a0d" :answer/id #uuid "192f30d7-4e5b-597c-b16e-383b0b641a0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbf3bc6d-a5c0-5131-be8c-1f828fbf1aa2" :answer/id #uuid "cbf3bc6d-a5c0-5131-be8c-1f828fbf1aa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efe3cfb8-92c2-5e3a-b874-10f960d8adca" :answer/id #uuid "efe3cfb8-92c2-5e3a-b874-10f960d8adca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8aba8f71-12dd-51aa-a5d5-f2bf983e166b" :answer/id #uuid "8aba8f71-12dd-51aa-a5d5-f2bf983e166b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ee90094-51ba-5008-b5eb-89abf0073014" :answer/id #uuid "9ee90094-51ba-5008-b5eb-89abf0073014" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107166 :tutorial/content "The **vector projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the portion that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends along $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\mathbf{a}|=2,$ $|\\mathbf{b}|=3,$ and the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nThe starting point of the vector projection is $O,$ and the ending point will be the point $P$ that lies on the line containing $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b267fb009e79eb16f86be42edc2c835808b28aa3eaf1c7e857300224ee01ec.png)\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ is called the **vector projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$ It is denoted as $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and it is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =OP\\cdot u \\\\  & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta$ is the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107181 :tutorial/content "When finding the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b},$ we sometimes find that the resulting projection vector points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}.$ This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/0e/0e973e6f6484a9aad863ebfe89fc1ea6356d6a50844931306c6aa2b63be18a8a.png)\n\nConsider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2,\\qquad |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=3,\\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nFirst, notice that the angle between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ is obtuse. This means we expect the projection vector to point in the opposite direction to that of $\\mathbf b.$\n\nNext, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the scalar projection is negative. \n\nThen, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b},$ as expected." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107168 :tutorial/content "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then by projecting $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$), we obtain the zero vector.\n\nFor example, the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto a perpendicular vector $\\mathbf{b}$ looks as follows:\n\n![](images/91/9163eda36b752f1e36195b8b5a1d40bf545ec21a3988f5480d6ddcbb926c00ea.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals the zero vector if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105967 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
